<!-- 1. PRECISION CRIMPING: THE FOUNDATION OF PRESSURE RETENTION -->
<section class="section" id="1-precision-crimping-the-foundation-of-pressure-retention">
  <h2>1. Precision Crimping: The Foundation of Pressure Retention</h2>
  <p>The crimping process is the primary mechanical operation that transforms a metal container into a pressurized delivery system. This stage involves the insertion of the valve into the can's 1-inch (25.4mm) opening, followed by the radial expansion of the valve cup. This expansion creates a permanent, airtight seal against the can’s "curling" or rim. Because the internal pressure of an aerosol can often exceed 5–8 bar at ambient temperature, the integrity of this seal is non-negotiable for shelf-life and consumer safety.</p>

  <p>What the crimp actually seals against is the valve gasket, so crimp settings can only be validated alongside the gasket material in use. A gasket that swells in contact with the formulation behaves differently under the same crimp dimensions than one that shrinks, which is why <a href="/aerosol-valve-gasket-explained" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">gasket material selection and chemical compatibility</a> is treated as part of the crimp specification rather than a separate decision. Where crimping is set up incorrectly, the resulting defects are the leading contributors to the failure modes described in our guide to <a href="/why-do-aerosol-cans-leak" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">why aerosol cans leak</a>.</p>

        <div class="table-container">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Crimp Parameter</th>
                        <th>Technical Description</th>
                        <th>Impact of Failure</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Crimp Diameter</td>
                        <td>The final external measurement of the expanded valve cup within the can opening.</td>
                        <td>Insufficient diameter leads to "valve blow-out" under high temperatures.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Crimp Depth</td>
                        <td>The vertical distance from the top of the can rim to the engagement point of the crimp.</td>
                        <td>Incorrect depth causes improper actuator seating or failure to dispense.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Gasket Compression</td>
                        <td>The percentage of deformation (target 15-25%) applied to the valve seal.</td>
                        <td>Inadequate compression allows micro-leakage of propellant over time.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

  <p>These three parameters are interdependent, so they are qualified together rather than one at a time. A crimp that measures correctly on a sectioned sample can still fail in service if tooling wears during the run, which is why dimensional checks are repeated at set intervals rather than only at set-up.</p>
</section>

<!-- 2. PROPELLANT GASSING: THE ENERGETIC COMPONENT -->
<section class="section" id="2-propellant-gassing-the-energetic-component">
  <h2>2. Propellant Gassing: The Energetic Component</h2>
  <p>Once the container is sealed, the propellant—the "engine" of the aerosol—must be introduced. This is achieved through high-pressure injection systems that force the propellant into the can. In modern manufacturing, this is typically done using "Through-the-Valve" (TTV) technology, where the gassing head depresses the valve stem and injects the propellant at pressures higher than the can's internal resistance. For high-volume production, "Under-the-Cup" (UTC) gassing is used, where the propellant is injected just before the crimping head seals the valve.</p>

  <p>The practical difference between the two methods is throughput. TTV must push the entire propellant charge through the narrow flow path of the valve stem and dip tube, which sets a hard ceiling on cycle time. UTC bypasses that restriction by dosing propellant into the open can in the fraction of a second before the cup is seated, so it supports far higher line speeds at the cost of more complex tooling and tighter timing tolerances.</p>

  <p>Propellant selection governs far more than spray force. It determines the flammability classification of the finished goods, the pressure the crimp must contain across the temperature range, and the solvency environment the gasket will live in. Liquefied hydrocarbon propellants are flammable, and facilities handling them fall under fire and explosion controls such as those published by the <a href="https://www.nfpa.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">National Fire Protection Association</a>. Where propellants are being switched for environmental reasons, the <a href="https://www.epa.gov/snap" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">EPA Significant New Alternatives Policy programme</a> lists which substitutes are acceptable for aerosol use. Our comparison of <a href="/aerosol-propellants-explained-lpg-vs-dme-vs-hfc-vs-n2-vs-co2" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">LPG, DME, HFC, nitrogen and CO₂</a> sets out the trade-offs in detail.</p>

        
