<!-- 3. AUTOMATIC WEIGHT CHECKING: THE QUALITY AUDITOR -->
<section class="section" id="3-automatic-weight-checking-the-quality-auditor">
  <h2>3. Automatic Weight Checking: The Quality Auditor</h2>
  <p>In an automated aerosol line, the weight check station serves as the final arbiter of success. Because the propellant is injected at high speeds, even a momentary drop in line pressure can result in an under-filled can. Conversely, an over-filled can is a safety hazard, as it may lack the "headspace" required for the propellant to expand if the can is exposed to heat. Modern check-weighers use electromagnetic force restoration to monitor every unit without stopping the production line.</p>

  <p>Headspace deserves particular attention because the physics are unforgiving. Liquids are effectively incompressible, so once a can is filled beyond its design level there is no vapour volume left to absorb thermal expansion. Internal pressure then climbs steeply with temperature rather than following the propellant's normal vapour pressure curve. This is why fill level is treated as a safety-critical parameter subject to continuous monitoring, not simply a commercial one.</p>

  <p>Because it inspects every unit rather than a statistical sample, the check weigher also doubles as a live process monitor. A slow downward drift in average fill weight points to a gassing pump losing pressure or an injector beginning to block, often long before any can falls outside the reject limit. Treating that trend data as an early warning signal is more valuable than using the station purely as a pass/fail gate.</p>
</section>

<!-- 4. LEAK DETECTION: THE SAFETY FIREWALL -->
<section class="section" id="4-leak-detection-the-safety-firewall">
  <h2>4. Leak Detection: The Safety Firewall</h2>
  <p>Aerosol cans are essentially pressure vessels. International transport regulations (such as ADR or DOT) and safety standards (like PESO in India) require that every single can produced be tested for leaks. This "safety firewall" prevents defective units from entering the supply chain, protecting both the consumer and the brand's reputation.</p>

  <p>The reference method is the hot water bath. Filled cans travel on a conveyor through heated water while operators or vision systems watch for escaping bubbles, and the same immersion simultaneously proves the can withstands elevated temperature without bursting. The <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A31975L0324" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">EU Aerosol Dispensers Directive 75/324/EEC</a> requires that the contents reach 50 °C during this test. Aerosols also travel as dangerous goods under UN 1950, and the packaging and pressure requirements for that classification are set out in the <a href="https://unece.org/transport/dangerous-goods" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">UN Model Regulations</a>; our guide to <a href="/dot-un-aerosol-transport-regulations-hazmat" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">DOT and UN aerosol transport rules</a> explains how these obligations apply to a finished batch.</p>

  <p>A bath test only catches leaks large enough to bubble within the residence time of the conveyor. Slower micro-leaks that develop over weeks are found instead through statistical weight-loss studies on retained samples, where cans are weighed repeatedly over a storage period. Running both controls together is what separates a line that ships reliably from one that passes end-of-line testing and still generates field complaints months later.</p>

        
